    faqs: [
      { q: 'Why has Veeva Vault become dominant in pharma?', a: 'Veeva Vault became dominant by solving a specific pharma problem: highly regulated industries (requiring validated, audit-trailed, Part 11-compliant document management) had limited cloud options before Vault. Veeva built the platform specifically for life sciences — with built-in GxP validation support, Part 11 compliance, and industry-specific workflows. The platform strategy (multiple applications sharing one platform) reduces integration complexity compared to point solutions from different vendors. Veeva\'s focus on life sciences — unlike Salesforce, Microsoft SharePoint, or OpenText which serve multiple industries — means faster industry-specific feature development.' },
      { q: 'Is Veeva Vault the same as Salesforce?', a: 'No — though Veeva Systems was initially built on the Salesforce platform (Veeva CRM runs on Salesforce), Veeva Vault is Veeva\'s proprietary platform built independently. Veeva announced in 2022 that it is migrating Veeva CRM from Salesforce to Vault CRM (its own platform) — completing the transition by 2025. Vault is not a Salesforce product and operates independently of Salesforce infrastructure. Veeva is a publicly traded company (NYSE: VEEV) serving life sciences specifically.' },
      { q: 'Can Vault eTMF and Vault CDMS work together?', a: 'Yes — one of Vault\'s key advantages is that multiple Vault applications share the same platform, enabling native integration. Vault eTMF and Vault CDMS can share study and site metadata (no duplication), safety data from Vault CDMS can flow to Vault Safety, clinical data from Vault CDMS can be linked to TMF documents in Vault eTMF, and user accounts are managed centrally across all Vault applications a company has purchased. This reduces data silos compared to organizations using Medidata Rave (EDC) + Florence (eTMF) + Argus Safety (PV) — three separate systems with separate user management and limited integration.' },
      { q: 'How do you get Veeva Vault training and certification?', a: 'Veeva offers multiple training options. Veeva Connect (connect.veeva.com) provides free self-paced training modules for all Vault applications — including Vault QMS, eTMF, RIM, and Safety. Vault certifications are available in Vault Platform, QualityDocs, QMS, RIM, eTMF, and Vault Safety — credentials valued in job postings. Veeva also offers formal instructor-led training for implementation teams. Third-party pharma training providers offer hands-on Vault training using sandbox environments. The fastest path to Vault proficiency: complete Veeva Connect certification training + seek project experience through a pharma employer or Veeva implementation partner.' },
    ],
