    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is ISO 13485 required for US FDA medical device approval?', a: 'ISO 13485 is not directly required by FDA for 510(k) or PMA approval — FDA has its own Quality System Regulation (21 CFR Part 820, now updated as QMSR). However, FDA\'s 2024 QMSR aligns with ISO 13485 to reduce regulatory divergence for companies operating globally. If a company is ISO 13485 certified, demonstrating QMSR compliance is significantly easier because the requirements are now substantially harmonized. For global medical device companies, ISO 13485 certification is effectively required to access EU, Canadian, Australian, and Japanese markets, even if it is not the specific FDA requirement.' },
      { q: 'What is the difference between a Notified Body and FDA?', a: 'FDA (US Food and Drug Administration) is the US government regulatory authority — it directly reviews and approves medical devices for the US market via 510(k), PMA, or De Novo pathways. A Notified Body (EU) is a private organization accredited by an EU member state to perform conformity assessments for CE marking — it audits manufacturers\' QMS and technical documentation but does not directly "approve" devices like FDA. EU MDR does not have a pre-market approval equivalent to FDA PMA for most device classes; instead, the Notified Body certifies the manufacturer\'s QMS and reviews clinical evidence.' },
      { q: 'What records must a medical device manufacturer maintain under ISO 13485?', a: 'ISO 13485 requires extensive records: Design History File (DHF — all design control records from development), Device Master Record (DMR — current device specifications, manufacturing processes, labeling), Device History Record (DHR — batch/lot records for each manufactured device unit), Supplier qualification records, Training records for all quality-relevant personnel, Internal audit records and CAPA records, Customer complaint records and regulatory reporting records (MDR/MAUDE for FDA), Post-market surveillance data, and Risk management file (ISO 14971 documentation). Records must be retained for the device lifetime + minimum 2 years after final production date (or as specified by applicable regulation — often longer).' },
      { q: 'How has EU MDR changed ISO 13485 requirements for device manufacturers?', a: 'EU MDR 2017/745 did not change ISO 13485 directly (ISO 13485 is an international standard), but it significantly increased requirements that ISO 13485-certified manufacturers must meet for EU market access. Key EU MDR additions: Stricter clinical evidence requirements (Clinical Evaluation Report must be more robust, equivalent devices must meet higher equivalence criteria), PMCF (Post-Market Clinical Follow-Up) obligations for all Class II+ devices, UDI (Unique Device Identification) implementation and EUDAMED registration, Summary of Safety and Clinical Performance (SSCP) for Class III and implantable devices, and enhanced Notified Body oversight. These additions are embedded into QMS procedures but go beyond what ISO 13485 alone requires.' },
    ],
